{#- ============================================================================
    v6 "why" — the client's card reference (cards.html, a Figma export) built
    onto the site's existing capacity content (c.capacity).

    The reference arrived as an absolutely-positioned Figma export: every card
    a fixed 218.96px wide with each element pinned at literal pixel offsets
    (left: 155.54px; top: 62.79px). That geometry cannot survive a fluid grid,
    so what is ported here is the *design* — anatomy, proportion, colour roles,
    the tilt on the last two cards — re-authored as flow layout. Nothing in
    this file or 08-v6-why.css positions a card's contents by absolute pixel.

    Card anatomy, per the reference:
      - a dark plate across the top of the stage: name + small uppercase
        qualifier on the left, one lime headline figure on the right
      - a white instrument overlapping it from below, leaving a band of the
        plate showing: a two-line heading with a lime badge beside it, then
        the widget
      - the section's own argument (h3 + paragraph) below the card, which the
        reference does not show because it is a component sheet, not a section

    Widgets, one per card, in reference order:
      1. chart  — yield bars, value called out above the two ends of the run
                  and the drought year, latest season picked out in lime
      2. bars   — two storage silos, dome-topped, filled to their own share
      3. lab    — protocol rows: name, reading, and a signed delta chip
      4. orbit  — distance rows: icon + stop, then a track with the km printed
                  on the fill and a marker at its end

    Card order is the reference's (chart, bars, lab, orbit), which swaps the
    last two against the order they sit in content/*.json. The swap is done
    here, by widget name, rather than by reordering the JSON: `capacity.cards`
    is also indexed positionally by the dispatch-radar geometry in
    tools/build_site.py and by the legacy #capacity macro in blocks.html.j2,
    and reordering the data would silently reach both.

    Self-contained: imports its own icon macro rather than assuming a caller
    has already bound `I`, since this file lives outside blocks.html.j2 and
    may be wired into the section dispatcher independently of it. `c`
    (content) and `iconic` (the [[icon]]-in-heading renderer registered as a
    Jinja global in tools/build_site.py) are expected from the render context,
    same as every other section macro.
    ============================================================================ -#}

{%- import "icons.html.j2" as I with context -%}

{%- macro section() -%}
{#- The reference draws the Kyiv leg with a mill rather than a generic route
    glyph — the stop is a mill, and 'route' is already spent on this card's
    own top badge, so reusing it would put the same mark twice in one card. -#}
{%- set stop_icons = {'auto': 'truck', 'rail': 'mill', 'port': 'ship'} -%}
{%- set badge_icons = {'chart': 'chart', 'bars': 'silo', 'orbit': 'route', 'lab': 'flask'} -%}
{#- Reference order, resolved by widget name so the JSON keeps its own. -#}
{%- set by_widget = {} -%}
{%- for card in c.capacity.cards %}{% set _ = by_widget.update({card.widget: card}) %}{% endfor -%}
{%- set ordered = [] -%}
{%- for w in ['chart', 'bars', 'lab', 'orbit'] %}{% if w in by_widget %}{% set _ = ordered.append(by_widget[w]) %}{% endif %}{% endfor -%}
{#- anything the reference order does not name still renders, at the end. -#}
{%- for card in c.capacity.cards %}{% if card not in ordered %}{% set _ = ordered.append(card) %}{% endif %}{% endfor -%}
<section class="section section--tight why-us" id="why" role="region" aria-labelledby="whyus-t">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag tag--lime" data-r>{{ c.capacity.eyebrow }}</span>
      <h2 id="whyus-t" class="h-iconic" data-split>{{ iconic(c.capacity.title_rich) }}</h2>
      <p class="lead" data-r>{{ c.capacity.lead }}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wcards">
      {%- for card in ordered %}
      {#- The reference leaves cards 1-2 square and tilts 3-4 in opposite
          directions. The tilt is a modifier rather than an :nth-child rule so
          it stays attached to the card that earned it if the order changes. -#}
      <article class="wcard{{ ' wcard--tilt-r' if loop.index == 3 }}{{ ' wcard--tilt-l' if loop.index == 4 }}" data-card>
        <div class="wcard__top">
          <span class="wcard__badge" aria-hidden="true">{{ I.icon(badge_icons[card.widget]) }}</span>
          <span class="wcard__num">{{ '%02d' % loop.index }}</span>
        </div>

        <div class="wstage">
          {#- Dark plate: the card's name and its one headline figure. Not
              aria-hidden — the figure lives nowhere else in the card. -#}
          <div class="wback">
            <div class="wback__l"><b>{{ card.head }}</b><span>{{ card.sub }}</span></div>
            <div class="wback__r"><b>{{ card.fig }}</b><i>{{ card.fig_unit }}</i></div>
          </div>

          <div class="wfront">
            <div class="wlabel"><span>{{ card.cap_k }}</span><em>{{ card.cap_v }}</em></div>

            {%- if card.widget == 'chart' %}
            {#- 01 — yield bars: value printed above the bar where the
                reference calls one out, drought year tagged, current season
                in lime. -#}
            {%- set peak = card.series | map(attribute='v') | max %}
            <figure class="cgbars">
              <figcaption class="sr">{{ c.a11y.chart_caption }}</figcaption>
              {%- for s in card.series %}
              {%- set is_dry = s.dry is defined and s.dry %}
              {%- set show_val = s.show_val is defined and s.show_val %}
              <div class="bcol{{ ' bcol--dry' if is_dry }}{{ ' bcol--now' if loop.last }}" style="--h:{{ (100 * s.v / peak) | round(1) }}%">
                {#- `decimal` is the locale's separator, from build_site.py.
                    Hardcoding a comma here printed "5,4" on the bars while
                    the plate above them printed "6.4" on the English page. -#}
                <div class="btrack">{% if show_val %}<b>{{ s.v | string | replace('.', decimal) }}</b>{% endif %}<i></i></div>
                <span>{{ s.year }}</span>
                {%- if is_dry %}<em class="btag">{{ card.dry_label }}</em>{% endif %}
              </div>
              {%- endfor %}
            </figure>

            {%- elif card.widget == 'bars' %}
            {#- 02 — two dome-topped silos, elevator + floor stores, each
                filled to its own share. Equal widths, per the reference: the
                fill height carries the split, the column width does not. -#}
            <div class="silos">
              {%- for r in card.rows[:2] %}
              {#- --f is set on the silo, not on the fill: the reading label
                  is a sibling of the fill and has to position itself off the
                  same number, so it must be able to inherit it. -#}
              <div class="silo{{ ' silo--dark' if loop.index0 == 1 }}" style="--f:{{ r.pct }}%">
                <div class="silo__v"><span class="silo__cap">{{ r.pct }}%</span><div class="silo__f"></div></div>
                <div class="silo__n"><b>{{ r.val }}</b>{{ r.name }}</div>
              </div>
              {%- endfor %}
            </div>

            {%- elif card.widget == 'lab' %}
            {#- 03 — protocol rows: name, reading, signed delta chip. A delta
                that reads as a drop is tinted differently from one that reads
                as a gain; both are in-spec, which is what the card says. -#}
            <div class="lspecs">
              {%- for r in card.rows %}
              <div class="lspec">
                <span class="lspec__k">{{ r.name }}</span>
                <span class="lspec__v">{{ r.val }}<i>{{ r.unit }}</i></span>
                <span class="lspec__d{{ ' lspec__d--neg' if '−' in r.trend or '-' in r.trend }}">{{ r.trend }}</span>
              </div>
              {%- endfor %}
            </div>

            {%- elif card.widget == 'orbit' %}
            {#- 04 — distance rows: icon and stop on one line, the track below
                it with the distance printed on the lime fill and a marker at
                the fill's end. -#}
            <div class="ladder">
              {%- for s in card.stops %}
              <div class="lrow">
                <span class="lrow__hd">
                  <span class="lrow__ic" aria-hidden="true">{{ I.icon(stop_icons[s.mode]) }}</span>
                  <span class="lrow__n">{{ s.name }}</span>
                </span>
                <span class="lrow__bar" style="--w:{{ s.w }}%">
                  <span class="lrow__track"></span>
                  <span class="lrow__fill"><em>{{ s.km }}&nbsp;{{ card.km_unit }}</em></span>
                  <span class="lrow__mk"></span>
                </span>
              </div>
              {%- endfor %}
            </div>
            {%- endif %}
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="wcard__h3">{{ card.title }}</h3>
        <p class="wcard__p">{{ card.text }}</p>
      </article>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{%- endmacro -%}
